# PORT-not-fixturize manifest.
#
# The differential-replay corpus (this crate) can express MANY of the engine's
# per-flow spec tests as data fixtures. But a class of spec tests CANNOT be
# expressed as a differential-replay fixture, because they assert properties
# that are invisible at the observable-I/O seam the corpus replays through:
#
#   * memory-zeroize / drop-witness / post-drop probe behaviour
#     (secret bytes are scrubbed on drop — not observable in wire output),
#   * footprint / size pins and other compile-fail (trybuild) goldens
#     (a type's size or a diagnostic — not a runtime observation),
#   * sole-path / staleness compile-and-probe properties
#     (a build-time guarantee or an arena-reuse staleness check).
#
# Those families MUST be kept as real tests (or rewritten in place) — never
# replaced by a corpus fixture, which could not carry the same guarantee, and
# never deleted. This file is the machine-checkable record of that rule: the
# `manifest_families_still_exist` test in tests/manifest.rs asserts every listed
# file is still present on disk, so an accidental deletion that removes one fails
# the gate loudly.
#
# This file is named `.list` (not `.txt`) on purpose: the repo's `.gitignore`
# ignores `*.txt` except the deps_pin goldens, so a `.txt` manifest here would
# be silently untracked. A non-`.txt` extension keeps it committed without
# editing the root ignore file.
#
# Format: one `family_name = relative/path/from/repo/root` per line; blank lines
# and `#` comment lines are ignored.
#
# The families that probed the retired push-path engine's internals were
# removed when that engine was deleted — the machinery they pinned no longer
# exists. Two of them carried properties that still matter on the sans-IO
# engine and were PORTED to real successor specs (registered below), not merely
# relabelled:
#   * buf_compact_staleness_spec  → engine_ingest_residue_spec. The old test
#     pinned a POSITIVE residue-scrub (ReadBuf::compact zeroed the abandoned
#     tail). IngestBuf replaces that with zero-once + a `filled` watermark:
#     the read side (`unread`) only ever sees `active[cursor..filled]`, so a
#     stale spare byte is never PUBLISHED (no per-read scrub needed). The new
#     spec proves that residue property behaviourally. (engine_ingest_memset_guard
#     is a DIFFERENT, complementary gate — a source-scan forbidding a per-read
#     memset on the hot path — not the residue property; the earlier note that
#     conflated them was imprecise.)
#   * fuzz_stress_spec            → engine_active_fuzz_spec. The randomised
#     xorshift byte-stream fuzzing (parse_header 100K + the active ingest
#     framer 10K) was lost in the cutover; engine_hostile_spec is STRUCTURED
#     (non-random) adversarial coverage, not a substitute. The new spec ports
#     the PRNG + iteration counts to the live targets.
# The remaining three had no surviving property to port:
#   * sole_path_compile_fail      → engine_linearity_compile_fail (construction sealing)
#   * error_arena_staleness_spec  → (error arena deleted; engine surfaces errors via EngineError)
#   * session_params_staleness_spec → (the PgProtocol Box-slot deleted; SessionParams the type stays)
# scram_fuzz_spec was REWRITTEN IN PLACE to drive the new connecting engine
# (its SCRAM-parser-robustness property is a carve-out, not push-path-specific).

scram_fuzz_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/scram_fuzz_spec.rs
scram_zeroize_miri_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/scram_zeroize_miri_spec.rs
zeroize_coverage_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/zeroize_coverage_spec.rs
footprint_drift_compile_fail = crates/postgres/proto/tests/footprint_drift_compile_fail.rs
secret_bounded_str_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/secret_bounded_str_spec.rs
engine_ingest_residue_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/engine_ingest_residue_spec.rs
engine_active_fuzz_spec = crates/postgres/proto/tests/engine_active_fuzz_spec.rs
